gt;&lt;td colspan="2" style="padding:1.5pt 0 1.5pt 1pt;text-align:right;vertical-align:bottom"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;(50.9)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td style="padding:1.5pt 1pt 1.5pt 0;text-align:right;vertical-align:bottom"/&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;&lt;tr&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="padding:1.5pt 1pt;text-align:left;vertical-align:bottom"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;Net payable to Evergy Generating&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td colspan="2" style="padding:1.5pt 0 1.5pt 1pt;text-align:right;vertical-align:bottom"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;(85.6)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td style="padding:1.5pt 1pt 1.5pt 0;text-align:right;vertical-align:bottom"/&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="padding:0 1pt"/&gt;&lt;td colspan="2" style="padding:1.5pt 0 1.5pt 1pt;text-align:right;vertical-align:bottom"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;(82.0)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td style="padding:1.5pt 1pt 1.5pt 0;text-align:right;vertical-align:bottom"/&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;&lt;tr&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="padding:1.5pt 1pt;text-align:left;vertical-align:bottom"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;Net payable to Evergy Industries&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td colspan="2" style="padding:1.5pt 0 1.5pt 1pt;text-align:right;vertical-align:bottom"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;(2.2)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td style="padding:1.5pt 1pt 1.5pt 0;text-align:right;vertical-align:bottom"/&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="padding:0 1pt"/&gt;&lt;td colspan="2" style="padding:1.5pt 0 1.5pt 1pt;text-align:right;vertical-align:bottom"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;(2.2)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td style="padding:1.5pt 1pt 1.5pt 0;text-align:right;vertical-align:bottom"/&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;&lt;tr&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="padding:1.5pt 1pt;text-align:left;vertical-align:bottom"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;Net receivable from Wolf Creek&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td colspan="2" style="padding:1.5pt 0 1.5pt 1pt;text-align:right;vertical-align:bottom"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;44.1&#160;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td style="padding:1.5pt 1pt 1.5pt 0;text-align:right;vertical-align:bottom"/&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="padding:0 1pt"/&gt;&lt;td colspan="2" style="padding:1.5pt 0 1.5pt 1pt;text-align:right;vertical-align:bottom"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;46.6&#160;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td style="padding:1.5pt 1pt 1.5pt 0;text-align:right;vertical-align:bottom"/&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;&lt;tr&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="border-bottom:2pt solid #000;padding:1.5pt 1pt;text-align:left;vertical-align:bottom"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;Net receivable from Evergy&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td colspan="2" style="border-bottom:2pt solid #000;padding:1.5pt 0 1.5pt 1pt;text-align:right;vertical-align:bottom"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;4.8&#160;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td style="border-bottom:2pt solid #000;padding:1.5pt 1pt 1.5pt 0;text-align:right;vertical-align:bottom"/&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="border-bottom:2pt solid #000;padding:0 1pt"/&gt;&lt;td colspan="2" style="border-bottom:2pt solid #000;padding:1.5pt 0 1.5pt 1pt;text-align:right;vertical-align:bottom"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;6.3&#160;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td style="border-bottom:2pt solid #000;padding:1.5pt 1pt 1.5pt 0;text-align:right;vertical-align:bottom"/&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;&lt;tr&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="display:none"/&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="display:none"/&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="display:none"/&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="display:none"/&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;&lt;/table&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:11pt;font-weight:700;line-height:120%"&gt;Affiliated Financing&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="margin-bottom:12pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:11pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;Evergy Kansas Central is authorized to participate in the Evergy, Inc. money pool, which is an internal financing arrangement in which funds may be lent on a short-term basis between Evergy Kansas Central, Evergy Kansas South, Evergy Metro, Evergy Missouri West and Evergy, Inc. Evergy, Inc. can lend but not borrow under the money pool.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="margin-bottom:12pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:11pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;As of June&#160;30, 2026, Evergy Kansas Central had a $93.6 million outstanding receivable from Evergy Kansas South and no outstanding payables under the money pool. As of December 31, 2025, Evergy Kansas Central had a $78.8 million outstanding receivable from Evergy Kansas South and no outstanding payables under the money pool.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:11pt;font-weight:700;line-height:120%"&gt;Tax Allocation Agreement&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="margin-bottom:12pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:11pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;Evergy files a consolidated federal income tax return as well as unitary and combined income tax returns in several state jurisdictions with Kansas and Missouri being the most significant. Income taxes for consolidated or combined subsidiaries are allocated to the subsidiaries based on separate company computations of income or loss. As of June&#160;30, 2026, and December 31, 2025, Evergy Kansas Central had income taxes receivable from Evergy of $5.6 million and income taxes payable to $10.5 million, respectively.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="margin-bottom:6pt;margin-top:12pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:11pt;font-weight:700;line-height:120%"&gt;TAXES (NOTE 11)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:11pt;font-weight:700;line-height:120%"&gt;Effective Income Tax Rates&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="margin-bottom:6pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:11pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;For the three months ended June&#160;30, 2026 and 2025, the effective income tax rates reflected in Evergy Kansas Central's financial statements were (3.9%) and (3.3%), respectively. The effective income tax rate decreased for the three months ended June&#160;30, 2026, compared to the same period in 2025, primarily due to an increase in equity allowance for funds used during construction (AFUDC) not includable in taxable income. &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="margin-bottom:6pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:11pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;Year to date June 30, 2026 and 2025, the effective income tax rates reflected in Evergy Kansas Central's financial statements were (3.6%) and (4.2%), respectively. The effective income tax rate increased year to date June 30, 2026, compared to the same period in 2025, primarily due to a decrease in the amortization of federal excess deferred income taxes (EDIT).&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;</ferc:DisclosureOfImportantDisclosuresRegardingTheFinancialStatementsTextBlock>
